e headline  um  yes there's a skills revolution i think  everything is always called a revolution  isn't it when a budget is announced and  border security  also sounds very let's please the red  wall-ish though um pretty patel doesn't  seem to be doing a terribly good job on  that front it was very interesting this  week that in fact it was actually  upsetting when sanji javid said that um  the number of deaths which had been you  know between 150 and 200 a day this week  were mercifully low but you know you get  20 people coming over in a boat from  france and that is you know a horrifying  number and something must be done but i  think this is the good bit of it for  rishi soon i call brand rishi as the  times insists on calling him because  this is when he gets to announce his  spending and have toddlers crawling  around number 11 and saying that you  know he's uh he's starting family helps  and all the rest of it the bad bit will  be afterwards when people realize a what  he hasn't spent money on on and b what  he's slashing and there's going to be an  awful lot of slashing coming up  uh ali do you share that sort of fairly  pessimistic view  well i do think that uh rishi sunak has  got a very narrow landing strip on which  to um uh navigate the situation on the  one hand  he's uh got various cabinet ministers  trumping at the bit for more money he's  got michael govind post uh in the new  department for leveling up he's also got  a new education secretary nadine zahawi  who are both pushing for increased  allocations to their budgets and he's  also very well aware of the fact that  he's got 400 billion of borrowing that's  been spent on the covid pandemic 2  trillion of public debt and the spectra  of rising inflation inflation already  above three percent predicted to go  above five percent in q1 next year and  now um murmuring is coming out of the  bank of england that interest rate rises  could be on the horizon as early as  november and that means for the  chancellor a 1 increase in interest  rates means 25 billion extra in interest  payments on government borrowing so he's  got a very difficult job and he works  for a boss um who we know  likes to have his cake and eat it who's  not really very focused on spending  issues so i don't think rishi sunak's  got an easy job to do i think it's  fraught with political difficulties for  him personally and we know that he's an  ambitious guy he's also a very smart and  clever guy who would like to probably  end up in number 10 himself so it's not  an easy situation for him to manage and  i think that we'll have to wait and see  what he does but it's not just a budget  it's also a three-year spending review  and i think everyone's going to be  watching that very very closely  uh yeah i see in the last few moments uh  we've just seen inside the sun wishi  sunak has written an article there  saying this week's budget and spending  review will set out a plan to deliver  the people's priorities support business  help our recovery ease pressures on the  cost of living but also to strengthen  the public finances so we can have a  stronger economy for the british people  at the angle the sunday telegraph is  going with christina is that the  chancellor is to spend billions to  digitize the nhs many nhs trusts still  using uh files and paper uh according to  the to the to the telegraph and actually  one thing um well one of the many things  i guess that that was a success in the  rollout of the vaccine initially was  that we managed to get things digitized  and up and running very quickly didn't  we  yes that was a huge nhs success and it  was you know slightly annoying that the  government kept claiming credit for it  because it was the nhs who did that and  who did as the phrase goes rather  irritatingly goes roll it out very  successfully i must say i think most of  us have seen big improvements on this  front in recent years you know i'm i  remember the first time i got cancer  they lost everything they lost um you  know all the results disappeared and i  went off to operate an operation and the  surgeon opened an envelope which was  meant to be a mammogram and turned out  to be an x-ray of an ankle so i do think  that things have improved since then and  now that we get texted by our gp surgery  and we get uh you know called for a flu  jab and things like that there certainly  is progress but it was alarming to read  in this telegraph piece that something  like one in ten trusts are still  paper-based which seems pretty  extraordinary in 2021 so this sounds  like a good idea but in terms of the  overall nhs budget it's going to be a  kind of drop in the ocean and yet again  there's mention in this piece about the  48 hospitals that boris johnson has  claimed that the tories will build well  we know that they've been everyone has  been told to call a new wing of a  hospital a new hospital so i think we  don't need to doubt that there will be  sort of orwellian twists to to make sure  that they pretend that they have met  their targets and kept their promises  which  seems unlikely given the massive  backlogs a massive waiting list that's  built up ali a quick thought on this  before we move on to covid  well look i actually think that the the  tories are out flanking labor on the nhs  now i mean what are you going to do if  you're on the left i mean the tories are  splashing money at the nhs the 1.25  percent increase in national insurance  which is going to be  uh  increasing the allocation to the nhs by  12 billion a year for the next three  years to deal with this massive nhs  backlog of five million people on  waiting lists which side of javits only  said a few weeks ago could rise to 30  million is a big big commitment my  biggest fear is that that money does not  get reallocated in three years time to  social care which is what it's meant for  this uh story is saying that five  billion is going to be spent on the  capital budget which